rationale:
The requirement `requirements.sdk.sources.local_provider` has one obligation. The SDK must supply a local-filesystem provider that lists and reads resources within an application-selected scope. It has no refining requirements. The only test marked for it is `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/sources.rs#fn[local_provider_scopes_paths_and_rejects_resources_outside_its_root]`. It covers each part of the obligation:

- **Listing:** `LocalSources::new(root)` with a default `SourceScope` lists every file under the root, and the test asserts the sorted locations `other/two.txt` and `src/one.txt`.
- **Application-selected scope:** a `SourceScope` with prefix `src` returns exactly one resource, which shows the scope narrows the listing. A search with prefix `.` also works.
- **Reading:** reading the listed resource returns the text `hit\n`. Parsing the same resource works through both the sync and async paths.
- **Stale reads:** the retained parse keeps its original bytes after the file changes, and a fresh parse sees the new contents.
- **Out-of-scope rejection:** the test asserts errors, not empty results, for these cases:
  - listing prefixes `..`, an absolute outside path, and a missing directory;
  - reading and parsing an absolute path outside the root;
  - reading an id whose scheme is `repo` but whose location is an outside absolute path;
  - on unix, listing through a symlinked directory that points outside the root;
  - on unix, reading a symlinked file that points outside the root.
- **Unknown resource:** reading an unknown id (`id("src/one.txt")`) fails.

The assertions check concrete outcomes, so a provider that returned outside files or ignored the scope would fail them. I found no uncovered obligation.
